## Why This Is Asked

Tests dynamic runtime schemas, non-blocking fetch, and routing on validation failure in KCNA pipelines.

## Key Concepts

- KCNA message with schemaId and payload
- Runtime schema fetch and TTL cache
- Per-message versioned validation and routing
- Safe error handling without blocking processing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: basic runtime validator setup using a cache and Ajv
class SchemaCache {
  constructor(fetcher, ttlMs = 60000) {
    this.fetcher = fetcher; this.ttl = ttlMs; this.store = new Map();
  }
  async get(version) {
    const entry = this.store.get(version);
    const now = Date.now();
    if (entry && (now - entry.ts) < this.ttl) return entry.validate;
    const schema = await this.fetcher(version);
    const Ajv = require('ajv');
    const ajv = new Ajv();
    const validate = ajv.compile(schema);
    this.store.set(version, { ts: now, validate });
    return validate;
  }
}

// usage sketch
const cache = new SchemaCache(async (v) => fetch(`/schemas/events/${v}.json`).then(r => r.json()));
async function onMessage(msg) {
  const { schemaId, payload } = msg.value;
  const validate = await cache.get(schemaId);
  const ok = validate(payload);
  if (ok) publish('validated_events', payload); else publish('invalid_events', payload);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test schema evolution with multiple concurrent versions?
- How would you handle schema fetch failures and guard against cache stampedes?

Why This Is Asked

Tests practical multi-tenant isolation, per-tenant offset management, and resilience in KCNA with simple backpressure and testing.

Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation and routing
- Token-bucket rate limiting per tenant
- Durable per-tenant offsets and recovery
- Idempotent processing with robust retries
- Lightweight test harness simulating downstream lag

Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: per-tenant rate limiter and offset store
class TenantState {
  constructor(tenant, rate) {
    this.tenant = tenant;
    this.offsets = new Map(); // topic -> offset
    this.limiter = new TokenBucket(rate); // per-tenant rate
  }
  canConsume() { return this.limiter.tryConsume(this.tenant); }
  commitOffset(topic, offset) { this.offsets.set(topic, offset); }
}
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate) {
    this.rate = rate; this.tokens = rate; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  tryConsume() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.last) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.rate, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.last = now;
    if (this.tokens >= 1) { this.tokens -= 1; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

Follow-up Questions

- How would you persist offsets across restarts and recover after crash?
- How would you scale the rate limiter across many tenants?
- What metrics would you collect to detect backlog per tenant?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes ability to design realistic, scalable A/B testing in KCNA with strict tenant isolation, deterministic sampling, and auditable results.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant data partitioning and sandbox isolation
- Deterministic per-tenant sampling and evaluation
